{% comment %}
  A curated size picker: some of a product option's values, in the merchant's
  own words.

  The product options block renders every value of every option, named as the
  product names them. That is right for a template serving a catalogue and wrong
  for a page selling one product, where the size is a decision to be framed — a
  90-day tub is "For the household", not "90 Day Supply", and a size the page is
  not selling this month should not appear at all.

  So this block takes four numbered slots. A slot names one value of the option
  and gives it a title and a subline; a slot left empty renders nothing. That is
  the whole mechanism for hiding a size: fill the two you sell and leave the
  third blank. Slot order is card order, so the row can be arranged without
  touching the product.

  Naming the option is enough to get a row. With no slot filled in, every value
  of the option renders under its own name — the block works the moment it is
  added, and the slots become a way to narrow and rename what is already there
  rather than a form that must be completed before anything appears. Filling one
  slot switches the row to exactly what the slots name, so curating never has to
  fight a fallback.

  Typed values are matched loosely enough to survive a merchant: case, outer
  space and the hyphen in "90-Day" are all normalised away, and it is the
  option's own spelling that gets written out, because the script matches
  variants on it exactly.

  It is a variant picker and nothing else. No plan, no price, no quantity — the
  offer block underneath prices whatever variant this lands on, which is what
  keeps the two from disagreeing about what is being bought.

  Hiding a value hides the control, not the variant: a shopper can still arrive
  on a hidden size through a link or a saved cart. The first card is marked
  selected in that case and the script resolves the variant to match it, so the
  page never shows a size that is not on the row.

  Accepts:
  - block {Object} - the size_option block. Reads settings.label,
    settings.option_name, settings.columns, and settings.opt1_value …
    settings.opt4_value with their _title, _subline and _badge.
  - product {Object} - product whose option values are read.
  - current_variant {Object} - variant currently selected.

  Usage:
  {% render 'CWC_pdp-size-option__block',
    block: block,
    product: product,
    current_variant: current_variant
  %}
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign wanted_option = block.settings.option_name | strip | downcase
  assign option_position = 0

  if wanted_option != blank
    for product_option in product.options_with_values
      assign this_name = product_option.name | strip | downcase
      if this_name == wanted_option
        assign option_position = product_option.position
        assign option_values = product_option.values
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  if option_position > 0
    assign option_key = 'option' | append: option_position
    assign selected_value = current_variant[option_key]
  endif

  assign slot_numbers = '1,2,3,4' | split: ','

  comment
    Whether the slots are being used at all. One typed value is enough: the row
    is then exactly what the slots name, and a value with no slot is a value the
    page has chosen not to sell.
  endcomment
  assign any_slot_typed = false
  for slot_number in slot_numbers
    assign value_key = 'opt' | append: slot_number | append: '_value'
    assign typed_value = block.settings[value_key] | strip
    if typed_value != blank
      assign any_slot_typed = true
    endif
  endfor
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The values to render, in the order they will appear.

  Built as text and split back apart because a slot's value has to be resolved
  against the option before it can be trusted — the merchant types the size, the
  option holds the spelling — and Liquid has no way to collect four resolved
  strings other than to write them out. The separator is deliberately something
  no option value would contain.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture curated_values -%}
  {%- if option_position > 0 and any_slot_typed -%}
    {%- for slot_number in slot_numbers -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign value_key = 'opt' | append: slot_number | append: '_value'
        assign typed_value = block.settings[value_key] | strip | downcase | replace: '-', ' '
      -%}
      {%- if typed_value != blank -%}
        {%- for option_value in option_values -%}
          {%- assign this_value = option_value | strip | downcase | replace: '-', ' ' -%}
          {%- if this_value == typed_value -%}{{ option_value }}~|~{%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  if any_slot_typed
    assign card_values = curated_values | split: '~|~'
  elsif option_position > 0
    assign card_values = option_values
  else
    assign card_values = '' | split: ','
  endif

  comment
    Which card starts lit. Normally the one the server rendered the page on; if
    that value is not on the row — a hidden size reached by link or by a saved
    cart — the first card takes it and the script resolves the variant to match.
  endcomment
  assign current_is_shown = false
  for card_value in card_values
    assign this_value = card_value | strip
    if this_value == selected_value
      assign current_is_shown = true
    endif
  endfor

  assign columns = block.settings.columns | default: 'two'
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Editor-only. A row that renders nothing is invisible in the theme editor as
  well, so without this the merchant sees an empty column and no reason for it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if request.design_mode and card_values.size == 0 -%}
  <div class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__editor-note" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
    <strong>No size cards are showing.</strong>
    {%- if block.settings.option_name == blank -%}
      Set Product Option to the option these cards choose.
    {%- elsif option_position == 0 -%}
      “{{ block.settings.option_name }}” is not an option on
      {% if product != blank %}“{{ product.title }}”{% else %}this product{% endif %}.
      {%- if product.options_with_values.size > 0 %}
        Options it has:
        {%- for product_option in product.options_with_values %}
          “{{ product_option.name }}”{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
        {%- endfor -%}.
      {%- else %}
        It has no options at all — every size picker needs one.
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- else -%}
      None of the Option Value fields match a value of “{{ block.settings.option_name }}”.
      Values it has: {{ option_values | join: ', ' }}. Clear them all to show every value.
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if card_values.size > 0 -%}
  <div class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__sizes-wrap" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
    {%- comment -%}
      The label and its chip share a line. The chip is a second way to pick the
      biggest size — the same choice the rightmost card offers, put where a
      shopper reading the question is already looking — so it says what it is
      worth rather than what it does, and only appears when there is more than
      one card to move between.

      It carries no data-cwc-variant. That attribute is what binds the option
      handler, and a chip carrying it would take the selection onto itself
      instead of onto a card; the script clicks the card for it instead.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if block.settings.label != blank or block.settings.upsell_text != blank -%}
      <div class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__sizes-head">
        {% if block.settings.label != blank %}
          <div class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__label">{{ block.settings.label }}</div>
        {% endif %}

        {% if block.settings.upsell_text != blank and card_values.size > 1 %}
          <button type="button" class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__upsell" data-cwc-size-upsell>
            {% if block.settings.upsell_icon != blank %}
              <span class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__upsell-icon" aria-hidden="true">
                {{- block.settings.upsell_icon -}}
              </span>
            {% endif %}
            <span class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__upsell-text">{{ block.settings.upsell_text }}</span>
          </button>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      data-cwc-flavor-position is the whole contract with the script: a row
      carrying it, holding controls that carry a value, is picked up by the
      click handler, the availability pass and the seed on load. Nothing about
      this block needs the script to know it exists.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__sizes cwc_product-hero-buy-box__sizes--{{ columns }}"
      data-cwc-flavor-position="{{ option_position }}"
    >
      {%- for raw_value in card_values -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign card_value = raw_value | strip

          comment
            The slot this value came from, and with it the copy. Found by asking
            the slots rather than carried along from the loop above, because the
            row may not have come from the slots at all — with none filled in
            every value renders, and every one of them lands here with no copy
            to find, which is the right answer rather than a missing one.
          endcomment
          assign card_prefix = ''
          if any_slot_typed
            assign match_value = card_value | downcase | replace: '-', ' '
            for slot_number in slot_numbers
              assign value_key = 'opt' | append: slot_number | append: '_value'
              assign typed_value = block.settings[value_key] | strip | downcase | replace: '-', ' '
              if typed_value != blank and typed_value == match_value
                assign card_prefix = 'opt' | append: slot_number | append: '_'
              endif
            endfor
          endif

          assign card_title = card_value
          assign card_sub = ''
          assign card_badge = ''

          if card_prefix != ''
            assign title_key = card_prefix | append: 'title'
            assign sub_key = card_prefix | append: 'subline'
            assign badge_key = card_prefix | append: 'badge'

            comment
              The value itself stays the title when none is typed. A slot with a
              value and no copy is a perfectly good card — it says what the
              product says.
            endcomment
            assign card_title = block.settings[title_key] | default: card_value
            assign card_sub = block.settings[sub_key]
            assign card_badge = block.settings[badge_key]
          endif

          comment
            Stock is read off the value alone rather than off the flavour the
            shopper is on: this is the state the page is rendered with, and the
            script re-runs it against the live combination as soon as anything
            is picked. The id is a seed for the same reason — the click handler
            resolves the real variant from every row at once.
          endcomment
          assign matches = product.variants | where: option_key, card_value
          assign in_stock = matches | where: 'available', true
          assign card_variant = in_stock.first | default: matches.first

          assign is_selected = false
          if card_value == selected_value
            assign is_selected = true
          elsif current_is_shown == false and forloop.first
            assign is_selected = true
          endif
        -%}

        <button
          type="button"
          class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__size{% if is_selected %} cwc_product-hero-buy-box__flavor--selected{% endif %}{% if in_stock.size == 0 %} cwc_product-hero-buy-box__flavor--unavailable{% endif %}"
          data-cwc-variant="{{ card_variant.id }}"
          data-cwc-flavor-value="{{ card_value | escape }}"
          {% if in_stock.size == 0 %}
            disabled
          {% endif %}
        >
          {% if card_badge != blank %}
            <span class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__size-badge">{{ card_badge }}</span>
          {% endif %}

          <span class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__size-title">{{ card_title }}</span>

          {% if card_sub != blank %}
            <span class="cwc_product-hero-buy-box__size-sub">{{ card_sub }}</span>
          {% endif %}
        </button>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
